-- Keyword searches to try on Discover and Search: the Cookie Policy says a keyword search of your uploads
-- also keeps their text on the device (lw-uploads), and What's new. src/data/legalPages.json and changelog.json
-- have the same. The Cookie Policy changes only while it still has the built-in wording.

update public.legal_pages set sections = $lw$[{"heading": "In short", "paragraphs": ["We use no advertising or analytics cookies, and we don't track you across other websites.", "Two kinds of cookie are set when you use LiteraryWorm, both by other companies: Cloudflare's bot check on our backend, and Google's cookies from books.google.com when Google Books covers and previews load.", "Your browser also keeps your sign-in, which device it is, your cookie choice, the app's own files and any books you save for offline. The list below says what each one is and how long it stays."]}, {"heading": "About this policy", "paragraphs": ["This Cookie Policy covers cookies and the similar things your browser keeps for LiteraryWorm, such as local storage and the app's offline caches. The Privacy Policy explains how we use the information itself.", "Some of what your browser keeps, such as your sign-in session, is linked to your account, so we treat it as personal information."]}, {"heading": "What are cookies?", "paragraphs": ["Cookies are small data files that are placed on your computer or mobile device when you visit a website. Cookies are widely used by website owners in order to make their websites work, or to work more efficiently, as well as to provide reporting information.", "Cookies set by the website owner (in this case, LiteraryWorm) are called \"first-party cookies.\" Cookies set by parties other than the website owner are called \"third-party cookies.\" Third-party cookies enable third-party features or functionality to be provided on or through the website (e.g., advertising, interactive content, and analytics).", "The parties that set these third-party cookies can recognize your computer both when it visits the website in question and also when it visits certain other websites."]}, {"heading": "Everything at a glance", "paragraphs": ["Here is everything that's set or kept in your browser when you use LiteraryWorm, who sets it and how long it stays. Each is explained further down.", "__cf_bm (a cookie, set by Cloudflare on .supabase.co, our backend): keeps out bots. Stays 30 minutes.", "Google's cookies (set by Google on books.google.com): set when Google Books covers and previews load, because we use the Google Books API. Google decides how long they stay; we can't read them.", "literaryworm-session (local storage): keeps you signed in. Stays until you log out.", "literaryworm-device (local storage): which of your devices this browser is, as a random id and your account id. Stays until you log out.", "TERMLY_API_CACHE (local storage, kept by Termly): your cookie choice. Stays up to 12 months.", "The app's files (the lw-shell cache): so the app opens quickly and offline. Replaced when the app updates.", "Books saved for offline (the lw-books cache): stay until you remove them, with a small note of your offline place and any listening schedule.", "Books you uploaded and opened, or searched by keywords (the lw-uploads cache): stay until you delete the book or log out.", "A push subscription, only if you turn on notifications on this device: stays until you turn them off or log out."]}, {"heading": "Why do we use cookies?", "paragraphs": ["Some cookies are required for technical reasons in order for our Website to operate, and we refer to these as \"essential\" or \"strictly necessary\" cookies. LiteraryWorm uses only essential cookies and similar technologies, including the third-party cookies Google sets from books.google.com when Google Books covers and previews load, because we use the Google Books API.", "We don't use cookies to track or target your interests, and we don't let third parties serve cookies through our Website for advertising or analytics. This is described in more detail below."]}, {"heading": "How can I control cookies?", "paragraphs": ["You have the right to decide whether to accept or reject cookies. Essential cookies cannot be rejected as they are strictly necessary to provide you with services.", "When you first visit, a banner from Termly, our consent provider, asks for your choice, and you can change it at any time with Consent Preferences at the bottom of every page. LiteraryWorm uses only essential cookies, so declining changes nothing about how the site works: book covers, audiobooks and your account work either way. If we ever add cookies that aren't essential, they'll only be used with your consent, and we'll update this page.", "You may also set or amend your web browser controls to accept or refuse cookies. Blocking third-party cookies stops Google's cookies, and LiteraryWorm keeps working. Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out and removes books saved for offline.", "The cookies set when you use our Website, and what each one does, are described below. Which ones are set can depend on the pages you visit: Google's, for example, only when a Google Books cover or preview loads."]}, {"heading": "Essential website cookies", "paragraphs": ["These cookies are strictly necessary to provide you with services available through our Website and to use some of its features, such as access to secure areas.", "__cf_bm. Purpose: Cloudflare places the cookie on end-user devices that access customer sites protected by Bot Management or Bot Fight Mode. Provider: .supabase.co (our backend, Supabase, which is protected by Cloudflare). Service: Cloudflare (see Cloudflare's privacy policy). Type: server cookie. Expires in: 30 minutes.", "Google Books cookies. Purpose: LiteraryWorm uses the Google Books API to find books and show their details. So the covers of books from Google Books, and the Google Books preview on some book pages (only when you open it), load in your browser from books.google.com, and Google sets and reads its own third-party cookies there.", "Provider: books.google.com (Google). Service: Google Books (see Google's Privacy Policy at policies.google.com/privacy). Type: third-party cookies, set by Google, not by LiteraryWorm; we can't read them.", "We mark Google Books covers and the preview as essential, so they show even if you decline other cookies in Consent Preferences. To stop Google's cookies, block third-party cookies in your browser's settings: LiteraryWorm keeps working, and covers still show. Kids profiles show only LiteraryWorm's own titles, so they don't load Google Books.", "The audiobook recordings streamed from the Internet Archive load from that service too, and it may set or read its own cookies under its own policy."]}, {"heading": "Similar technologies: what we keep in your browser", "paragraphs": ["Signed in: when you log in, your browser keeps your sign-in session (in local storage, under literaryworm-session) so you stay signed in after a refresh. It's strictly necessary for your account to work, and it's removed when you log out.", "Next to it the app keeps which of your devices this browser is (under literaryworm-device: a random id and your account's id, nothing else), so a refresh or a second tab isn't listed as a new device in Listen on another device; it's removed when you log out too. Passkeys you add are kept by your device, browser or password manager, not by LiteraryWorm in your browser's storage, and logging out doesn't remove them.", "Your cookie choice: Termly keeps the choice you make in the banner, a random identifier for this browser and a count of visits in local storage (under TERMLY_API_CACHE), for up to 12 months, so the banner doesn't ask again on every visit. It sets no cookies. Your browser loads the banner from Termly (app.termly.io) on each visit, so Termly receives those requests, including your IP address, under its own privacy policy.", "The app itself: so LiteraryWorm opens quickly and without a connection, your browser keeps a copy of the app's own files (its pages, code, icons and app manifest). This holds nothing about you. If you turn on notifications in Account → Preferences, your browser also keeps a push subscription for LiteraryWorm; turning them off or logging out removes it.", "Books you save for offline: when you choose Read offline, the book's text, and its audio if you ask, are kept on your device until you remove them from the book's page.", "If you turn on Listen offline on a schedule for an audiobook, the app also keeps a small note of that choice (how many chapters, Wi-Fi only, whether to remove finished ones) with the books, and saves or removes chapters as you listen; it goes when you turn it off or the books are cleared.", "Next to them the app keeps a small note of which account's offline place this device holds (a random id, not your name or email), so the place can be freed from another device; it goes when the books do.", "Books you upload: once you've opened one, or searched your uploads by keywords, its text is kept on that device so it keeps working if your connection drops. It's removed when you delete the book or log out.", "Everything else, such as reader settings, playback speed, the demo household and uploads made in the demo, stays in the open tab only and is gone when you refresh or close it.", "Your library, reading progress, bookmarks, highlights, saved words, lists, Up next queue, ratings, reading time and pace, quiz scores, player settings, club activity and uploaded books are saved with your account on our servers (Supabase), not in browser cookies. The Privacy Policy says how we use them, and Account lets you download or delete them.", "Listening on a speaker or TV uses your browser's own Chromecast or AirPlay picker: it loads no script, sets no cookie and keeps nothing in your browser, and the speaker plays the recording straight from the Internet Archive."]}, {"heading": "What else your browser loads from other sites", "paragraphs": ["Some parts of LiteraryWorm load straight from other companies' servers, so your browser sends them requests, with your IP address, and they handle them under their own policies.", "Google Fonts (fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com) serves the app's typefaces. Google Books (books.google.com) serves covers and the preview, and sets the cookies described above.", "The Internet Archive (archive.org) streams LibriVox recordings and audio clips. Termly (app.termly.io) serves the consent banner.", "When you pay, you go to Stripe's own checkout page, where Stripe sets its own cookies under its privacy policy. We never see your card details.", "OpenDyslexic, the reading font, is part of the app itself, so choosing it sends nothing to anyone."]}, {"heading": "Do my choices apply on all my devices?", "paragraphs": ["No. Cookie settings you make in your browser, and anything you clear there, apply to that browser on that device only. You'll need to make them again on a different device or browser, and a private or incognito window deletes what it kept when you close it.", "Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out, and books saved for offline will need saving again."]}, {"heading": "How can I control cookies on my browser?", "paragraphs": ["As the means by which you can refuse cookies through your web browser controls vary from browser to browser, you should visit your browser's help menu for more information.", "That's true of the most popular browsers: Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and Opera. In each, you can block third-party cookies for every site, or clear the cookies and site data of one site.", "We don't use advertising networks, so there's nothing to opt out of here. If you'd like to opt out of targeted advertising elsewhere, most advertising networks offer a way to: see the Digital Advertising Alliance, the Digital Advertising Alliance of Canada or the European Interactive Digital Advertising Alliance."]}, {"heading": "Local network access", "paragraphs": ["Your browser may ask whether LiteraryWorm can reach devices on your local network, such as others on your home Wi-Fi.", "Choose Block: the app never needs it, and allowing it can stop logging in from working in that browser. If you allowed it, turn it off in the site's settings (in Chrome and Edge, select the icon beside the address, then Site settings, and set Local network access to Block)."]}, {"heading": "What about other tracking technologies, like web beacons?", "paragraphs": ["Cookies are not the only way to recognize or track visitors to a website. Web beacons (sometimes called \"tracking pixels\" or \"clear gifs\") are tiny graphics files that contain a unique identifier that lets a website recognize when someone has visited it or opened an email including them.", "LiteraryWorm doesn't use web beacons or tracking pixels on the Website or in its emails. We send our emails with Mailgun's open and click tracking turned off."]}, {"heading": "Do you serve targeted advertising?", "paragraphs": ["No. We don't serve advertising on our Website, and we don't allow third parties to place cookies or web beacons on your computer or mobile device through our Website to serve or measure advertising. Google's cookies from books.google.com are set under Google's own policies; we don't use them, and we don't receive them."]}, {"heading": "How often will you update this Cookie Policy?", "paragraphs": ["We may update this Cookie Policy from time to time in order to reflect, for example, changes to the cookies we use or for other operational, legal, or regulatory reasons. Please therefore revisit this Cookie Policy regularly to stay informed about our use of cookies and related technologies.", "The Last updated date at the top of this Cookie Policy shows when it last changed. When a change matters, we'll also say so on What's new."]}, {"heading": "Where can I get further information?", "paragraphs": ["If you have any questions about our use of cookies or other technologies, please email us at support@literaryworm.com. For privacy requests, email usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}]$lw$::jsonb, updated_at = now()
