m Termly, our consent provider, asks for your choice, and you can change it at any time with Consent Preferences at the bottom of every page. LiteraryWorm uses only essential cookies, so declining changes nothing about how the site works: book covers, audiobooks and your account work either way. If we ever add cookies that aren't essential, they'll only be used with your consent, and we'll update this page.", "You may also set or amend your web browser controls to accept or refuse cookies. Blocking third-party cookies stops Google's cookies, and LiteraryWorm keeps working. Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out and removes books saved for offline.", "The cookies set when you use our Website, and what each one does, are described below. Which ones are set can depend on the pages you visit: Google's, for example, only when a Google Books cover or preview loads."]}, {"heading": "Essential website cookies", "paragraphs": ["These cookies are strictly necessary to provide you with services available through our Website and to use some of its features, such as access to secure areas.", "__cf_bm. Purpose: Cloudflare places the cookie on end-user devices that access customer sites protected by Bot Management or Bot Fight Mode. Provider: .supabase.co (our backend, Supabase, which is protected by Cloudflare). Service: Cloudflare (see Cloudflare's privacy policy). Type: server cookie. Expires in: 30 minutes.", "Google Books cookies. Purpose: LiteraryWorm uses the Google Books API to find books and show their details. So the covers of books from Google Books, and the Google Books preview on some book pages (only when you open it), load in your browser from books.google.com, and Google sets and reads its own third-party cookies there.", "Provider: books.google.com (Google). Service: Google Books (see Google's Privacy Policy at policies.google.com/privacy). Type: third-party cookies, set by Google, not by LiteraryWorm; we can't read them.", "We mark Google Books covers and the preview as essential, so they show even if you decline other cookies in Consent Preferences. To stop Google's cookies, block third-party cookies in your browser's settings: LiteraryWorm keeps working, and covers still show. Kids profiles show only LiteraryWorm's own titles, so they don't load Google Books.", "The audiobook recordings streamed from the Internet Archive load from that service too, and it may set or read its own cookies under its own policy."]}, {"heading": "Similar technologies: what we keep in your browser", "paragraphs": ["Signed in: when you log in, your browser keeps your sign-in session (in local storage, under literaryworm-session) so you stay signed in after a refresh. It's strictly necessary for your account to work, and it's removed when you log out.", "Next to it the app keeps which of your devices this browser is (under literaryworm-device: a random id and your account's id, nothing else), so a refresh or a second tab isn't listed as a new device in Listen on another device; it's removed when you log out too. Passkeys you add are kept by your device, browser or password manager, not by LiteraryWorm in your browser's storage, and logging out doesn't remove them.", "Your cookie choice: Termly keeps the choice you make in the banner, a random identifier for this browser and a count of visits in local storage (under TERMLY_API_CACHE), for up to 12 months, so the banner doesn't ask again on every visit. It sets no cookies. Your browser loads the banner from Termly (app.termly.io) on each visit, so Termly receives those requests, including your IP address, under its own privacy policy.", "The app itself: so LiteraryWorm opens quickly and without a connection, your browser keeps a copy of the app's own files (its pages, code, icons and app manifest). This holds nothing about you. If you turn on notifications in Account → Preferences, your browser also keeps a push subscription for LiteraryWorm; turning them off or logging out removes it.", "